// Family: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== family.f01 | feature | journey
title: The Book Woman's Mule
genre: family + adventure
tone: Muddy, warm and funny; hard times told without self-pity
era: 1936 | period
setting: The hollows of eastern Kentucky | real | mountain
logline: An eleven-year-old girl takes over her injured mother's pack-horse library route for one week, with a mule who knows the way better than she does and a mountain that has decided it does not need books.
premise: The government pays a dollar a day to women who will carry library books by horse to cabins no road reaches. {birdie}'s mother has broken her leg. If the route is missed for a week the county gives it to someone else. {birdie} can read, more or less, and {junior} the mule has done the eighteen miles two hundred times.
synopsis: {birdie} has never wanted the route; she wants to be at the picture show in town. Her mother, {ruth}, gives her the saddlebags, the ledger and one rule: you do not judge a house by what it borrows. The first day {junior} stops at every cabin on his own and will not be hurried past one. She meets the route as her mother's customers: a widower who wants only seed catalogues; a schoolroom of twelve with one primer; twin boys who hide the books so they will not have to give them back. The mountain's quarrel is with {granny_tolliver}, who can doctor anything and holds that reading weakens the eyes and the will, and whose word closes doors. On the third day the creek is up and {junior} refuses the ford; {birdie} forces him and loses a saddlebag of books to the water. She has to tell twenty households their books are gone. Nobody is angry, which is worse. A miner's boy, {caleb}, who cannot read and has been pretending, walks the next stretch with her and she finds herself teaching him the way her mother must have taught half the hollow. {granny_tolliver}'s own grandchild is sick; the remedy she half remembers is in a book {birdie} carries, a home-doctoring manual, and somebody has to read it to her aloud. {birdie} does, by lamplight, and is not thanked. She is, the next morning, handed a recipe written out in a shaky hand to be pasted in the scrapbook that goes round the route. She brings the ledger home complete. Her mother asks how it was. {birdie} says the mule did most of it, and asks if she can do Thursday.
question: What do you owe a job you inherited and did not choose?
themes: reading as something carried; pride and need; a child learning her mother's worth
audience: Families; children of eight and up
viewpoint: With {birdie}, and occasionally with a very patient mule
stakes: The family's dollar a day, the route itself, and twenty households' only books
conflict: A girl who would rather be anywhere else against weather, distance and one formidable old woman
ending: The route is kept. {granny_tolliver} contributes a page. {birdie} asks for Thursday.
experience: Laughter at a mule's expense, a pang at a lost saddlebag, and the glow of being useful
look: Fog in hollows, split-rail fences, a girl on a mule against a ridge; books wrapped in oilcloth
sound: Hooves in creek gravel, a dulcimer, pages. A mule's opinion of everything.
m: keep | community | self-vs-others | restored
@ birdie | Birdie Mae Combs | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 11 | To get the week over | She had other plans | Eighteen miles, a flood and a mule with views | Impatient, sharp, kinder than she lets on | From doing a chore to wanting the route | Kentucky quick-talk, sasses the mule | Her mother's hat, too large; overalls
@ junior | Junior | co-lead | ally | animal | he/him | - | To do the route the way it is done | He has done it two hundred times | A child who thinks she is in charge | Stubborn, dependable, wise about creeks | From ignoring her to waiting for her | Ears, mostly | A grey mule with saddlebags and a rope halter
@ ruth | Ruth Combs | supporting | mentor | human | she/her | 34 | The route kept until her leg mends | It is the only wage in the house | She has to trust an eleven-year-old | Tired, proud, good at it | From giving orders from a bed to asking how it was | Low, even, saves her words | Splinted leg, a ledger on the quilt
@ granny_tolliver | Granny Tolliver | supporting | antagonist | human | she/her | 78 | The hollow kept as it was | Everything new has taken something from her | She cannot read, and her grandchild is sick | Fierce, capable, never wrong | From shutting the door to adding a page | Proverbs, flat refusals | Black bonnet, herb sack, a walking stick used for pointing
@ caleb | Caleb Ison | featured | foil | human | he/him | 12 | Not to be found out | Everyone thinks he can read | The book woman's girl is quicker than her mother | Proud, funny, a liar from embarrassment | From pretending to sounding out a line | Brags; goes quiet over print | Miner's cap too big, bare feet
